    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothair-ii.jpg" alt="Lothair II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothar_II,_King_of_Lotharingie.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ekkehard von Aura, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> takes its name from Lotharingia, the middle kingdom created for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothar II</a> at the partition of the Carolingian empire in 855, which stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> to the Jura. After passing between the West and East Frankish kings, it was attached to the German kingdom in 925 and governed as a duchy. In 959 Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruno-the-great">Bruno of Cologne</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a> and duke of Lotharingia, divided it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Lower Lorraine</a> in the north and Upper Lorraine along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> and upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, entrusting the south to Frederick, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-montenegro">Bar</a>. Frederick’s house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> held Upper Lorraine for most of the next century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothelo-i-duke-of-lorraine">Gothelo I</a> briefly reunited both duchies between 1033 and 1044. After his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-iii-duke-of-lower-lorraine">Godfrey the Bearded</a> rebelled against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III</a>, the emperor gave Upper Lorraine in 1047 to Adalbert and, after Adalbert’s death in 1048, to his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-alsace">Gerard of Alsace</a>. Gerard’s descendants ruled for almost seven centuries, while Lower Lorraine fragmented into Brabant, Limburg and other principalities and the name Lorraine came to mean the southern duchy alone. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> and the counts of Bar ruled substantial territories around it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nancy">Nancy</a> became the usual ducal residence from the twelfth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sarreguemines"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarreguemines.jpg" alt="Sarreguemines" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sarreguemines">Sarreguemines</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarreguemines2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fepinedar, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> lay on the linguistic frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: French, in its Lorraine dialects, was spoken in most of the duchy, while the north-eastern <em>bailliage d’Allemagne</em> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarreguemines">Sarreguemines</a> spoke German dialects. Most people were peasants growing grain and vines in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> valleys, with pasture and forest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>. The dukes drew much of their revenue from salt: brine springs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dieuze">Dieuze</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateau-salins">Château-Salins</a> and Rosières supplied Lorraine and neighbouring lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> mines in the Vosges, notably around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sainte-marie-aux-mines">Sainte-Marie-aux-Mines</a>, and forest glassworks added to ducal wealth in the sixteenth century. The duchy sat on routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, Champagne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the fairs and pilgrimage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicolas-de-port">Saint-Nicolas-de-Port</a>, where a great basilica was begun in 1481, drew merchants and pilgrims. Lorraine remained firmly Catholic: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco">Charles III</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-pont-a-mousson">Jesuit University of Pont-à-Mousson</a> in 1572, and the dukes repressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. The duchy was also among the regions of Europe worst affected by witch trials around 1600, when the ducal prosecutor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-remy">Nicolas Rémy</a> claimed to have condemned some nine hundred people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">The Thirty</a> Years’ War brought armies, plague and famine that killed a large share of the population.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy.jpg" alt="Battle of Crécy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_crecy_froissart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Loyset Liédet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dukes commanded modest forces drawn from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and towns, supplemented in the early modern period by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and their position between the French and German worlds exposed the duchy to every great conflict of the region. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raoul-duke-of-lorraine">Raoul</a> was killed fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> in 1346, and in 1431 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, who claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> through his wife Isabella, was defeated and captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bulgneville">Battle of Bulgnéville</a> by his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoine-of-vaudemont">Antoine de Vaudémont</a> with Burgundian help. The duchy’s greatest military moment came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-ii-duke-of-lorraine">René II</a>, whose lands lay across the corridor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> needed to link his territories. Charles occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nancy">Nancy</a> in 1475, but René retook it and, with Swiss and Alsatian troops, defeated and killed Charles before the city on 5 January 1477. In 1525 Duke Antoine destroyed a peasant army of the German Peasants’ War near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saverne">Saverne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War Charles IV led a capable mercenary army in imperial and Spanish service, but France occupied the duchy from 1633 and razed the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mothe-en-bassigny">La Mothe</a> after its siege in 1645. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>’s armies returned in 1670, and Leopold had to accept the demolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>’s fortifications when he recovered his duchy in 1697.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor,_seated_(1500%E2%80%931558),_formerly_attributed_to_Titian_(Alte_Pinakothek,_Munich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dukes were princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, but the duchy’s ties to the Empire were loose and contested. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>’s own institutions included the Assizes of the old knighthood (<em>Assises de l’ancienne chevalerie</em>), a court in which the old noble families judged cases and checked ducal power, and Estates General that voted taxes until they ceased to meet after 1629. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nuremberg-1542">Treaty of Nuremberg</a> of 1542 between Duke Antoine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> recognised Lorraine as a free and non-incorporable duchy with only limited obligations to the Empire. The dukes also held the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-montenegro">Bar</a>, united with Lorraine under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-ii-duke-of-lorraine">René II</a> after 1480, although the western Barrois beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> had been held in fief of the French crown since 1301, which gave French kings a legal foothold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco">Charles III</a>, raised at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a>, built a more centralised princely state: he extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> with the planned Ville-Neuve from 1588, issued a general codification of Lorraine customary law in 1594 and enlarged the ducal bureaucracy. A younger son of René II founded the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-guise">Guise</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> during the Wars of Religion and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-guise">Mary of Guise</a>, mother of Mary, Queen of Scots. Leopold restored orderly government after 1697 from his new palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/place-des-vosges"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/place-des-vosges.jpg" alt="Place des Vosges" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/place-des-vosges">Place des Vosges</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_des_Vosges_vue_a%C3%A9rienne.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Des Racines et des Ailes, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>’s most lasting legacy is dynastic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor">Francis Stephen</a>, the last duke of the house, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1736 and became Emperor Francis I in 1745; their descendants, the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">Habsburg-Lorraine</a>, ruled Austria until 1918 and Tuscany until 1859. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-of-lorraine">Cross of Lorraine</a>, adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-ii-duke-of-lorraine">René II</a>, later became the emblem of the Free French under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-guise">Guise</a> branch shaped the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The duchy was also a remarkable artistic centre. The printmaker <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-callot">Jacques Callot</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nancy">Nancy</a> recorded the horrors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War in <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-miseries-and-misfortunes-of-war">The Miseries and Misfortunes of War</a></em> (1633), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-de-la-tour">Georges de La Tour</a> worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a>, and the landscape painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-lorrain">Claude Lorrain</a>, born at Chamagne, took his name from his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy</a> on the borders of Lorraine and Champagne and visited Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-lorraine">Charles II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> in 1429. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-leszczynski">Stanisław Leszczyński</a> the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-here">Emmanuel Héré</a> and the ironworker <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-lamour">Jean Lamour</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/place-des-vosges">Place Royale</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/place-stanislas">Place Stanislas</a>, in 1752–1755, one of the finest urban ensembles of eighteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The duchy’s position between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> foreshadowed the later contest over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession.jpg" alt="War of the Polish Succession" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession">War of the Polish Succession</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_Polish_Succession_Collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LocalF15, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>’s independence was eroded by French expansion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> in 1552, encircling the duchy with French garrisons. Charles IV’s alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII</a>’s rebellious brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-duke-of-orleans">Gaston of Orléans</a>, who secretly married the duke’s sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-countess-of-comminges">Marguerite</a>, provoked French invasion in 1633; the duchy was occupied for most of the next three decades and ravaged by war, plague and famine. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vincennes-1661">Treaty of Vincennes</a> of 1661 returned it on harsh terms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied it again from 1670 until the Treaty of Ryswick restored Duke Leopold in 1697. Leopold rebuilt the economy, but the duchy had become a pawn of great-power diplomacy. To end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession">War of the Polish Succession</a>, France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> agreed in 1735–1738 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor">Francis Stephen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>’s intended husband, would exchange Lorraine for the succession to the Grand Duchy of Tuscany, which he received in 1737. Lorraine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-montenegro">Bar</a> went for life to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-leszczynski">Stanisław Leszczyński</a>, the deposed king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and father-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, but by the secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-meudon">Declaration of Meudon</a> of 1736 he left the government to a French intendant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoine-martin-chaumont-de-la-galaiziere">Antoine-Martin Chaumont de La Galaizière</a>. When Stanisław died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a> in February 1766, Lorraine was annexed to the Kingdom of France.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> shows how a frontier principality between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> survived for eight centuries, largely under one dynasty, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> and sustaining a brilliant court culture, before French pressure absorbed it and its last duke founded the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">Habsburg-Lorraine</a> that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</em></p>

